    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2026 EICMA's Speed Legends Exhibit Was Not To Be Missed – See the Bikes That Paved MotoGP History\nToyota Recalls 19,000 Electric C-HR Crossovers, Battery Overcharge Condition To Blame\n7 photos\nPhoto: Toyota\nThe world's largest automaker is recalling an estimated 19,000 examples of the C-HR+ worldwide. Known as C-HR in North America due to the lack of an internal combustion alternative in said part of the world, the full-electric sport utility vehicle may experience an overcharge condition during regenerative braking due to a software fault in the EV control unit.\nAccording to Kraftfahrt-Bundesamt, namely the German Federal Ministry for Transport, vehicles produced between June 17, 2025, and July 9, 2026, feature the subject EV control unit software. Said unit manages the electric drive system of the zero-emission crossover, which is produced at the Takaoka plant for all markets. Takaoka is also the home of the mechanically related Subaru Uncharted.\nToyota explains that EV-ECU software can allow a nearly full high-voltage battery to overcharge during regeneration operation, making this a battery management issue. Regeneration operation, meanwhile, is when energy is recovered by lifting off the accelerator or pressing the brake pedal. Regenerative operation, therefore, feeds energy back into the high-voltage battery.\nLithium-ion batteries for EV applications have a limited charge window, and if the EV-ECU software doesn't manage the flow of energy correctly, the battery can exceed its safe operating range. When this happens, overcharging triggers a bunch of warning messages and shuts down the electric drive system. The loss of motive power at high speeds understandably increases the risk of a crash.\nThe German market accounts for 1,096 of the affected vehicle population, and the KBA further highlights that said campaign is under investigation. Authorized Toyota dealers will gladly reflash the subject control unit free of charge, yet the Japanese automaker has not published a whole lot of relevant details for European models, including when the remedy software will become available.\nPhoto: Toyota Thankfully, we do know this problem has two North American counterparts in the US and Canadian markets. The US recall covers 8,521 units of the C-HR, with production dates ranging between February 6, 2026, and July 10, 2026. Its filing describes the very same fault and remedy, with Toyota Motor North America further mentioning that said problem was discovered as part of internal testing.\nSo far, the company reported zero warranty claims and field reports potentially related to the described condition. Over in Canada, recall number 2026421 covers 1,311 units of the 2026 model year C-HR electric sport utility vehicle. Said campaign was issued on September 2, 2026, and replaces an earlier safety recall.\nAs noted earlier, C-HR+ is what its maker calls the model in the Old Continent, making it easy to distinguish from the internal-combustion alternative. By internal combustion, yours truly is referring to either a hybrid or a plug-in hybrid. These models pack either 1.8- or 2.0-liter naturally aspirated I4s. The C-HR+ is underpinned by a different platform, specifically the e-TNGA rather than TNGA-C.\nAdvertised with 150-kW DC fast-charging capability, the C-HR+ for Europe offers two battery and three powertrain options. Base means 57.7 kWh and 123 kilowatts going to the front wheels alone, whereas the mid-range version ups the ante to 77 kilowatt-hours and 165 kilowatts. The top configuration adds an 88-kW rear motor, resulting in 343 ps (338 hp) and 438 Nm (323 lb-ft) of torque.\nAvailable from nearly 35,000 euros in Germany, the C-HR+ is listed with anything from 458 to 607 kilometers (285 to 377 miles) in the WLTP on a full charge. Its battery pack, be it the smaller or larger unit, comes with a 10-year warranty.\nVia // KBA\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\ntoyota c-hr Toyota recall EV\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 1,400-HP Twin-Turbo C8 Corvette Z06 Runs 9.0 Quarter-Mile, Grenades From Too Much Extra Torque",
